  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>On Aug 11–12, SNSC chief Mohsen Rezaei held his first formal engagement as security council secretary — a meeting with Chinese Ambassador Cong Peiwu — and issued a public statement setting six conditions for Hormuz reopening: cessation of US military operations against Iran and its regional allies, termination of the naval blockade, withdrawal of US forces from Iran's vicinity, full war-damage compensation, lifting of all sanctions, and release of approximately $24bn in frozen Iranian assets. In the same statement, Rezaei explicitly separated any navigation route text finalized with Oman from the broader commercial reopening of the strait — a public decoupling that removes the pathway under which F1's resolution criteria could be satisfied before the Aug 19 deadline without a fundamental reversal of both declaratory positions. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span> (0.25 → 0.08)</p>

      <p>On Aug 12, Trump posted on social media that the US holds "total control" over the Strait of Hormuz via a "wall of steel" naval blockade; US Central Command reported redirecting 59 commercial vessels, disabling three, and boarding two in a single day's enforcement. Iran's government formally rejected Trump's characterization. ABC News reported from Tehran that no ceasefire extension discussions have taken place. The simultaneous hardening of both declaratory positions — SNSC setting a maximalist six-point floor, Trump asserting full operational dominance — compressed the space for a compromise text before the MOU 60-day negotiating window closes Aug 16. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span> (0.35 → 0.18)</p>

      <p>Pakistan Interior Minister Naqvi traveled to Tehran on Aug 12 for what Pakistani and regional media described as a "final truce push" ahead of the Aug 16 MOU expiry. He met President Pezeshkian and FM Araghchi. Al Jazeera reporting on Aug 12 confirmed Iran held firm on all conditions; no extension framework was announced. The MOU text specifies the 60-day window is "extendable with mutual consent" — mutual consent requires the US to accept Iran's conditions or Iran to retreat from Rezaei's six-point floor, and neither has occurred. Naqvi's visit is the last documented mediator contact ahead of the Aug 16 deadline. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, new <span class="cite">N1</span> (MOU extension, 3-day, p=0.15)</p>

      <p>Lloyd's List Hormuz Brief dated Aug 12 recorded 78 commercial transits for the week Aug 3–9 — approximately 11 vessels/day on a 7-day moving average — down from 95 the prior week (~13.5/day). JMIC threat level for the Hormuz zone remained at SUBSTANTIAL as of Advisory Update 082 dated Aug 9, with no further downgrade since the Aug 5 SEVERE→SUBSTANTIAL movement. Iranian kinetic activity on the Omani coast approaches continued through the reporting period, and AIS/GPS jamming makes ~11/day a conservative lower bound. At that rate the <span class="cite">F3</span> threshold of 40/day is 3.6× the current volume, with all leading indicators static or deteriorating. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span> (0.25 → 0.12)</p>

      <p>Iran's parliamentary National Security and Foreign Policy Committee approved the Hormuz governance bill framework unanimously on Aug 9 — zero opposing votes, an outcome Iran International described as unusual even for politically charged legislation — and the Internal Affairs Committee simultaneously cleared a provision barring US and Israeli vessel transit with fines at up to 20% of cargo value. A full plenary vote and Guardian Council review remain before the bill becomes law. If the bill clears the plenary — the threshold for new forecast <span class="cite">N2</span> at p=0.55 on a 48-day horizon — it encodes in Iranian domestic legislation the conditions Rezaei has stated as SNSC policy, constraining the room available to any future Iranian executive in a comprehensive deal negotiation. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (0.25 → 0.15)</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
