  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Within Iran's decision-making structure, the clearest development of this cycle is Mohsen Rezaei's consolidation of the SNSC around an explicitly IRGC-aligned Hormuz doctrine. Rezaei — IRGC commander-in-chief for 16 years, then senior adviser to Khamenei — chose his first formal engagement as SNSC secretary to meet the Chinese ambassador rather than an Oman or Qatar mediator. The sequencing communicates a priority order: Beijing before Muscat before Washington. Rezaei stated in July that control of the Strait of Hormuz is worth "dozens of atomic bombs" — a formulation that frames the closure as a permanent strategic capability rather than a temporary bargaining instrument, and that forecloses the kind of partial confidence-building reopening that mediators had been working toward. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The parliamentary committee's unanimous approval of the Hormuz bill on Aug 9 — zero opposing votes across two parallel committee tracks — is the legislative expression of the IRGC-aligned policy position. Both the National Security and the Internal Affairs committee passages cleared without any amendment softening the US and Israeli vessel-barring provision. The trajectory represents an institutional effort to bind future Iranian governments to the closure in domestic law before any negotiated reopening agreement can take effect. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>A visible but unresolved internal division persists between the Pezeshkian administration's diplomatic track — Araghchi's continued technical engagement with Oman, willingness to receive Pakistan's Naqvi — and the SNSC/IRGC track that Rezaei now controls. Araghchi on Aug 9 used the phrase "exchange of messages" to characterize US-Iran communication, distancing himself from the harder SNSC formulation without challenging it. Pezeshkian met Naqvi on Aug 12 and his office framed the visit as supportive of mediation. Rezaei's six conditions are the operative policy floor for any resumption of formal engagement, and no Iranian official in this 48-hour cycle publicly contradicted them. The Oman mediation committee has held no sessions since Iranian strikes on Oman in July (~18 Omani nationals killed, 23 injured); Oman continues to describe itself as an active mediator without scheduling further sessions — the channel remains formally open and operationally inactive. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

